## Run it

Unzip the export and work inside its folder. Everything the script writes (checkpoint, errors,
report, a downloaded `odb`) stays in that folder, wherever you run it from.

```bash
unzip {{.Folder}}.zip
cd {{.Folder}}
{{- range .EnvExample}}
export {{.}}
{{- end}}
./migrate.sh run         # checks everything first, then migrates; progress is printed as JSON lines
```
{{if .Sealed}}
The script asks for the export passphrase (or set `ODB_BUNDLE_PASSPHRASE` to run unattended).
{{- end}}{{if eq .TargetKind "directory"}}
The JSON files go to `{{.TargetPath}}`, as set in the project; set `ODB_TARGET_PATH` to write elsewhere.
{{- end}}

- If the run stops (Ctrl+C, a reboot, a failure), `./migrate.sh resume` continues from the last
  checkpoint in `$ODB_STATE_DIR` (default `odb-state` in this folder).
- `./migrate.sh run` also continues from that checkpoint, so after a completed run it writes
  nothing, even if you deleted the migrated {{if eq .TargetKind "directory"}}files{{else}}documents or collections{{end}}. To migrate everything
  again, run `./migrate.sh run --restart`: it moves the old checkpoint, errors and report to
  `archive/<time>/` in the state directory and starts over.
- `./migrate.sh preflight` runs only the checks, writing nothing.
- `./migrate.sh status` summarizes the run; `./migrate.sh verify` counts the documents in the target.
- `./migrate.sh inspect` shows what the script will do; `./migrate.sh extract` writes
  `bundle.odb.json` for use with `odb run --bundle` directly{{if .ConfigFile}} (add `--config config.json`){{end}}.
- Pass `odb run` options after `--`, for example `./migrate.sh run -- --throttle 5000`.
{{- if .ConfigFile}}

### Run it against another environment

The source{{if .TargetHasCredentials}} and target{{end}} connection details are in `config.json`, not in the script. To
run the same migration elsewhere, or after a password change, edit that file and run again;
there's no need to export again.

- Each section (`source`{{if .TargetHasCredentials}}, `target`{{end}}) lists the connection's `type`, its settings
  (host, port, database, user…) and its `password`. The `type` can't change.
- Passwords are kept as `"base64:…"`. To change one, replace the whole value, prefix
  included, with the new password in plain text: the next `preflight`, `run`, `resume` or
  `verify` encodes it and saves `config.json` again.
- `ODB_CONFIG=/path/to/other.json ./migrate.sh run` uses another file, e.g. one per environment.
- A checkpoint follows the migration, not the connection: use another `ODB_STATE_DIR` for each
  environment.
{{- end}}

### Settings

| Variable | Default | Meaning |
|---|---|---|
| `ODB_STATE_DIR` | `odb-state` in this folder | Checkpoint, `errors.jsonl` and `report.json` |
{{- if eq .TargetKind "directory"}}
| `ODB_TARGET_PATH` | `{{.TargetPath}}` | Folder the JSON files are written to |
{{- end}}
| `ODB_RUNTIME` | `{{.Runtime}}` | `auto`, `binary`, `docker` or `embedded` |
{{- if .ConfigFile}}
| `ODB_CONFIG` | `config.json` in this folder | Source and target connection details |
{{- end}}
{{- if .Sealed}}
| `ODB_BUNDLE_PASSPHRASE` | (asked) | Passphrase of sealed credentials |
{{- end}}
| `ODB_BIN` | | An `odb` {{.Version}} binary to use |
| `ODB_DOCKER_ARGS` | | Extra `docker run` arguments, e.g. `--network host` |
| `ODB_CACHE_DIR` | `odb-cache` in this folder | Where a downloaded `odb` is kept |

With `auto`, the script uses `ODB_BIN`, then {{if .EmbeddedPlatform}}the `odb` embedded in it ({{.EmbeddedPlatform}}), then {{end}}`odb` {{.Version}} on the `PATH`, then Docker (`{{.Image}}`), and finally downloads `odb` from `{{.DownloadURL}}`, checking it against the checksum written into the script.

## Exit codes

| Code | Meaning |
|---|---|
| 0 | Completed |
| 2 | Completed with errors, below the error threshold: see `errors.jsonl` |
| 3 | Failed: error threshold exceeded or a fatal error; see `report.json` |
| 4 | Not started: preflight or configuration problem (bad bundle{{if .ConfigFile}} or `config.json`{{end}}, schema drift, wrong {{if .Sealed}}passphrase{{else}}credentials{{end}}, target not writable) |
| 5 | Cancelled after saving the checkpoint: run `./migrate.sh resume` |

After the run, import `report.json` from the state directory into the Omni-Data-Bus project
(Review → Export… → Import report) to see the production results next to your test runs.
